    },
    "q-87484": {
      "dom_id": "step-q87484",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "The graph of $y=2f(3x+6)+1,$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Translate up by $1$ unit\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Translate left by $2$ units\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}5)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=2f(3x+6)+1=2f(3(x+2))+1\n$$\n\nTo get $y=2f(3(x+2))+1,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4).$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(3x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2).$ Translate $y=f(3x)$ left by $2$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(3(x+2)).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}3).$ Stretch $y=f(3(x+2))$ by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=2f(3(x+2)).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}1).$ Translate $y=2f(3(x+2))$ up by $1$ unit to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=2f(3(x+2))+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}1).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
